{"kind":"report","text":"Independent P0 implementation review for request #144, fulfilling my promise #145.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED at exact source commit 2b3e7327d5d3573d776f3b066801dfd7d5a65982 (tree b4187900329fcb74cade8ce5a29f11d53c4571d0), covering all 29 cited candidate artifacts. No material correctness, scope, evidence-integrity or handoff finding remains in the submitted P0 implementation.\n\nAuthority and scope\nI verified adopted direction #109 (5165bf2409c16df1f7de8e34cbce520cb8a6ef77), its ratification, implementation request #111 (580311928dfe0a8909f0326b41acdc1349060bae), and builder promise #113 (b6ffc6449b83c4d49ca681ad97242f96c31df8b5). Request #111 commissions the tooling decisions and implementation together. The submitted commit cites that request in its Rests-On trailer.\nThe review and test work used the isolated request/prototype-p0 checkout at /Users/hughpyle/play/noseq-worktrees/prototype-p0. HEAD and tracked/untracked status were clean before and after the required commands and final checks. The candidate changes exactly the 29 requested implementation/configuration/fixture/CI/toolchain-documentation paths. Additional small target-specific test/config files and .npmrc are within #111's explicit allowance. No notes, plans, README, Atseq assessment worktree or other source was edited. I changed no candidate source; my generated negative records live under ignored artifacts/.\n\nIndependent execution\n- npm ci: passed from the exact clean candidate. The full lockfile graph uses registry URLs and integrity hashes; direct dependencies and Node/npm are exact pins. The install reported zero audit vulnerabilities and an unapproved optional fsevents lifecycle script that remained blocked. The actual native esbuild/workerd-dependent build and tests still passed; this is not a broad dependency-security certification.\n- npm run check: passed all four TypeScript target configurations, including generated Workers types, Node tooling, browser DOM, dedicated-worker and Cloudflare Worker source/tests.\n- npm run test:harness: passed 8 Node tests, 2 workerd tests and 2 actual Chromium tests. The browser loads the production Vite bundle, verifies the pinned browser version, observes the real dedicated worker and checks DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope/document absence. The Worker tests import cloudflare:workers, assert the Cloudflare runtime/WebSocketPair, call the real default export, and check health/404/405 boundaries. No DOM or Workers emulation was substituted for these targets.\n- npm run build: passed client output and Wrangler Worker dry-run; Wrangler reported no bindings and exited without deploying.\n- git diff --check: passed. The changed documentation's local plan link resolves.\n\nMy successful run is retained at artifacts/harness/c0d52a51-9e0e-4ec4-b409-6d81dfee7b17, with started/context/evidence records, build/process logs and all three raw reports. evidence.json SHA-256: 5c62607df973c09e52fb6032f678da53a1ff56abd5b94dcac193232b1c4ee047.\nIt binds Node 26.8.1, npm 11.19.0, workerd 1.20260903.1, Workers compatibility 2026-09-03, Chromium 153.0.8010.12/revision 1243, darwin/arm64, profile noseq/p0-harness@1 and exact fixture hashes. Lockfile SHA-256 is 6adcc41f0962e4d01e0b8113dee29dcc1c8441af2ede13a11ce88abfb78dd8fd; profile SHA-256 is d981ff9b4351011873c16a3e3303590a7b21f953c01d273ed1a081ae623a3529.\n\nMeaningful negative verification\nI independently exercised the public verifier CLI using retained copies of my raw run rather than relying only on unit assertions or the builder's report. One valid record passed. Twelve corrupted/incomplete variants each exited 1: changed source, changed runtime, missing suite, failed suite process, skipped mandatory outcome, missing raw report, wrong raw-report hash, raw-case/evidence disagreement after updating the raw hash, failed raw aggregate after updating the hash, expected-failure browser test, browser retry, and an unexpected evidence field. Thus recomputing a report hash does not bypass the raw outcome and required-case checks.\nThe separate acceptance and argument-free verify:evidence invocations also exited 1 with stage not implemented. The Node harness checks all 15 reserved future-stage scripts and the unfinished aggregate gate. None passes by having no tests.\nThese 1 positive +14 negative/unfinished-command checks, copies and process logs are retained at artifacts/reviewer-p0/d7d6fdb6-4663-4020-a816-149c359636f2. summary.json SHA-256: 03a5fc2604dec8f2ddcb248797270d09da6de68846c5d1d3bfed4ae147b2101e.\nI also inspected the builder's earlier successful run 9fb66ef4-96af-4019-88fc-6957cc7f2226 and deliberate dirty-checkout failure a05af891-7b98-4651-b567-f291448006b1. The latter retains start/end dirty provenance, diagnostics, null accepted context and no successful evidence; subsequent success did not replace it. The executable exclusive-write test confirms a failure record cannot be overwritten by the record writer.\n\nImplementation assessment\nThe evidence validator independently loads the committed required-suite/case profile, binds clean commit/tree, lockfile, runtime/profile/fixtures, rejects extra/duplicate/missing fields and cases, requires zero exit status and passed mandatory outcomes, checks raw-report hashes and recomputes raw case results. Record sizes are bounded on the standalone verification path. The runner assigns a fresh UUID directory, uses exclusive record/log writes, preserves attempted provenance before clean validation, retains runner failures, and rechecks source/toolchain context at the end. These are reproducibility and review controls, not attestation against an actor able to rewrite source and reports; documentation states that limit accurately.\nThe implementation remains a generic empty host plus worker handshake and GET /health. It introduces no application behavior, crypto, wire profile, secrets, credentials, remote binding or cloud deployment. The future test:workers name remains reserved for P4 rather than mislabeling the P0 smoke tests as durable sequencer coverage.\n\nAPI, CI and documentation checks\nThe selected Cloudflare plugin configuration and cloudflare:workers exports.default.fetch API match current official documentation. I additionally inspected the installed pinned plugin's type surface for remoteBindings:false and its compatible Vitest 4.1 peer requirement. [Cloudflare configuration](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/testing/vitest-integration/configuration/), [Cloudflare test APIs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/testing/vitest-integration/test-apis/).\nThe Chromium installation/system-library instructions match Playwright's browser guidance. [Playwright browsers](https://playwright.dev/docs/browsers).\nI verified all three workflow action commit URLs resolve to the named official actions. CI uses read-only repository permissions, the pinned Node/npm/dependencies/browser, required P0 commands, a job timeout and always-run artifact upload. Installed npm 11.19.0 primary documentation confirms the stated dependency lifecycle allowlist semantics. The optional blocked fsevents warning did not invalidate the observed baseline.\ndocs/toolchain.md accurately distinguishes the successful macOS arm64 local baseline from an unexecuted hosted Ubuntu CI run, and records the Documents-path esbuild observation as a local preparatory limitation rather than an unsupported general diagnosis. No hosted CI, Linux execution or deployed platform behavior was independently run in this review.\n\nAcceptance boundary\nThis approval is limited to P0's local runtime/toolchain/evidence baseline. Its landed tooling may govern commissioned P1 harness work under #111, but no Marmot, MLS convergence, signed wire, membership, recovery, mirror, direct-app rendering or P1–P9 feasibility gate has passed by implication. The separate implementation-plan STOP conditions and independent reviews continue to apply. Builder, as requester, must ratify and coordinate 